WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.177
Hi, I'm Elder!

00:00:02.177 --> 00:00:04.541
Jev picks one of the options you send.

00:00:04.541 --> 00:00:07.200
Here's how, and two separate reasons its answer moves.

00:00:08.642 --> 00:00:09.698
Jev samples nothing.

00:00:09.698 --> 00:00:14.275
Yet resending a question moves its answer; reordering the options moves it more.

00:00:19.008 --> 00:00:22.713
You send a question, its evidence, and a menu of options:

00:00:22.713 --> 00:00:24.061
seventy-seven, in this benchmark.

00:00:24.481 --> 00:00:26.914
Jev scores them in one forward pass.

00:00:26.914 --> 00:00:30.390
No seed, no temperature: nothing is drawn token by token.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.545
Out comes a probability for every option, rounded to two decimals.

00:00:34.965 --> 00:00:37.665
The answer is always one you sent.

00:00:37.665 --> 00:00:40.366
That's the no-hallucination claim: closure, not correctness.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.847
A letter readout picks a token.

00:00:43.847 --> 00:00:48.391
On one open model, slot A alone was worth about one point seven logits.

00:00:48.811 --> 00:00:51.259
A per-option scorer rates each option alone.

00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:55.106
Shuffle the menu and only the rows shuffle: no order effect.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.113
Not sampling: there's nothing to sample.

00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:02.456
Not the version: every response named one pinned model.

00:01:02.876 --> 00:01:06.020
Not the client: identical input tokens, every time.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.090
What's left is serving: batches, kernels, the fleet.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:13.727
Reasoned guess: if Jev is a mixture of experts, routing depends on the batch.

00:01:15.258 --> 00:01:19.130
That open project's scorer on a Mac: same fixture and pinned checkpoint

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:20.743
as its published CUDA run.

00:01:21.163 --> 00:01:22.070
Every winner agrees.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:25.699
Scores move by up to point one oh five: an eighth of

00:01:25.699 --> 00:01:27.211
what reversing the options buys.

00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:30.572
The published thirteen percent pools both.

00:01:30.572 --> 00:01:35.679
Split: twelve items flip with the order, five with nothing changed, four with both.

00:01:37.017 --> 00:01:40.641
The answer is always one you offered, but not a property

00:01:40.641 --> 00:01:41.958
of the question alone.

00:01:41.958 --> 00:01:43.935
Average a few orderings before thresholding.

00:01:46.192 --> 00:01:50.446
To recap: one pass, two kinds of drift, one published number holding both.

00:01:50.866 --> 00:01:52.879
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:53.454
I'm Elder.

00:01:53.454 --> 00:01:53.742
Bye!
